Problem Statement 
TBX — BVP Tech Catalyst Hackathon 
Problem Statement Title: Build a Finance Assistant That Actually Understands You 
1. Background 
• Finance teams and business users rely on dashboards, static reports, and exports to answer routine questions 
about their own financial data. 
• Getting a simple answer, such as what was spent on a vendor last month, or which transactions are still 
unreconciled, usually means finding the right report, learning its terminology, or waiting on someone in 
finance ops to run a query. 
• This slows down everyday decisions and pulls finance teams away from higher value work, since they end 
up fielding the same lookup questions on repeat. 
• A conversational assistant that understands plain language and answers directly from real data would let 
anyone get a trustworthy answer in seconds, without opening a dashboard. 
• The stakes are higher here than in most chatbot use cases. A wrong or invented number in a finance context 
is not a minor bug, it is a liability that can undermine reconciliation, audits, and trust in the numbers. 
2. Challenge 
Build a conversational AI assistant that lets users ask questions about their financial data in plain language and get 
clear, accurate answers back. Examples: 
• How much did we spend on vendor payouts last month? 
• Which transactions are still unreconciled? 
The assistant should answer instantly, without the user touching a dashboard. Grounding is the crux of this 
challenge: every answer must come from real data, never from the model filling in a plausible-sounding number. 
3. Requirements 
Must Have 
• Natural language query handling: accept free-form questions through a chat interface and correctly interpret 
intent, filters, and date ranges. 
• Grounded retrieval: every answer must be produced by querying the provided financial dataset 
(transactions, vendor payouts, reconciliation status), not from the model's own assumptions. 
• Accurate computation: filter, group, and aggregate the data correctly (by vendor, date range, status, 
category) before handing results to the language model, so the model explains a computed result rather than 
calculating one itself. 
• Verifiable answers: every response should pair a plain language answer with the underlying records or a 
breakdown table, so the user can check it. 
• Hallucination guardrails: when the data needed to answer a question doesn't exist, or the question is 
ambiguous, the assistant should say so clearly rather than invent a figure. 
• Lightweight model constraint: see Section 7, Assumptions and Constraints. This is a scored requirement, 
not a suggestion. 
• Multi-turn conversation: follow-up questions, like asking how that compares to the month before, should 
work without the user repeating context. 
• Explainability: the assistant should be able to show what data it pulled and how it arrived at an answer, not 
just state the answer, so a user can trace the reasoning back to the source records. 
Good to Have 
• Export of the underlying breakdown to CSV or Excel. 
Bonus 
• Confidence signalling: the assistant flags when it is less certain about an answer instead of stating 
everything with equal confidence. 
• A short note on model choice: which lightweight model was used, why, and what accuracy looked like 
against a sample question set. 
• Simple anomaly callouts, for example flagging a vendor payout that looks unusually large compared to 
history while answering the original question. 
Out of Scope 
• Live integration with real banking or ERP systems 
• Multi-tenant security, user roles, or production-grade authentication 
• Support for every possible financial question. A well-scoped subset (spend, payouts, reconciliation) is 
enough 
4. Resources Provided 
• We will share a starter dataset covering transactions, vendor payouts, reconciliation status, chart of 
accounts, vendor list, and a data dictionary before the hackathon. 
• A sample chart of accounts and vendor list to give the data structure 
• A short guidance note on how the model-efficiency constraint will be evaluated 
• We will provide each team with equal, capped API credits so access is not a deterrent. Free or locally 
hosted open-source models are also allowed. 
5. Evaluation Criteria 
• Accuracy & grounding 30% 
• Model efficiency 20% 
• Natural-language understanding 15% 
• Functionality 15% 
• User experience 10% 
• Presentation 5% 
• Business impact 5% 
6. Submission Requirements 
• Working prototype (chat interface plus backend) 
• Architecture diagram 
• README with setup instructions 
• Sample questions and the corresponding answers produced by the assistant 
• Presentation deck covering the problem, approach, model choice rationale, and demo flow 
7. Assumptions & Constraints 
Constraint: Lowest possible model, highest possible accuracy 
Build this using the smallest model that can still deliver accurate answers. Bigger is not better here. 
Teams will be judged on how well they get a lightweight model to perform, not on how much compute 
they throw at the problem. Defaulting to the largest available frontier model, without justification, will be 
scored down. 
Other Mandatory Constraints 
• All answers must be grounded in the provided data schema only. No fabricated figures. 
• The data schema is scoped to a single fictitious company in a single currency. 
• We are defining a limit of 20M records in the database for the prototype to be tested 
• We have decided the Upper limit for LLM - 20B parameter. 
Not Expected 
• Live integration with real banking or ERP systems 
• Multi-tenant security, user roles, or production-grade authentication 
• Support for every possible financial question. A well-scoped subset (spend, payouts, reconciliation) 
is enough